  "query_preview": "..\n\nRest of this afternoon through Friday: A vigorous shortwave is \nrounding a deep upper level low pressure system off to our south. \nThe center of the low is situated over central California and the \nshortwave is currently pushing across northern Nevada. A highly \ndiffluent flow pattern aloft and a strong vorticity maximum in the \nnortheast quadrant of the this low pressure system will be the \nkicker that initiates convection across the Inland Northwest today. \nStrong to severe thunderstorms are already being observed in western \nIdaho to eastern Oregon. The potential for severe thunderstorms will \ninitially favor the southern Idaho Panhandle into southeast \nWashington by 3-4 pm. Thunderstorms are then progged to develop \nnorth to northwest with time through the rest of the afternoon into \nthe evening. Thunderstorms will initially favor supercell \ndevelopment with the potential for hail of 1.5-2.0 inches and along \nwith damaging outflow winds. There is a non-zero chance for \ntornadoes with supercells over the southeast portion of the forecast \narea; a very low chance of 2-4 percent within a 25 mile radius, but \nnot of the question this afternoon.\n\nThunderstorms are then expected to organize along a line and \ntransition to favoring damaging winds over producing large hail as \nthis line progresses across the Columbia Basin into the Spokane- \nCoeur d'Alene Area. The amount of CAPE in the 1000-3000 J/kg range \nwith 20-30 kts of 0-6 km bulk shear is enough for the potential of \ndamaging wind gusts along the leading edge of the line of \nthunderstorms. As the cold pool gets established, we could see winds \nin the 60-70 mph range. This will not only bring the potential for \ndown trees and power lines, but also bring the potential for blowing \ndust across the Columbia Basin, into the Palouse and northward \nalong the Highway 2 corridor. Blowing dust may be enough to \nreduce visibility down to a mile or less and result in rapid \nchanges to visibility.\n\nThe line of thunderstorms is expected to push north of the Canadian \nborder between midnight to 2AM. A shortwave pushing in off of the \neastern Pacific will bring additional convection on Friday primarily \nacross the northern mountains, but also across the southern Idaho \nPanhandle. We won't be nearly as unstable as today, but there is \nstill the potential for a few stronger thunderstorms that brings \nsmall hail, gusty outflow winds, and brief heavy downpours. The \nshortwave will also tighten the westerly gradient with gusty winds \nof 35-45 mph through the Cascade gaps into the Wenatchee Area, \nWaterville Plateau, and into the western Columbia Basin.\n\nFriday night through Thursday: The disturbance that moves \nacross on Friday looks to push much of the moisture and \ninstability east of the region by Saturday. It also will bring \ntemperatures down a bit with temperatures back down to near \nnormal in the 60s to low 70s. Overall a much drier forecast for \nthe weekend and occasionally breezy. Showers and isolated \nthunderstorms may hang on over the Idaho Panhandle into the \nNortheast Mountains through the weekend into Monday as the \nshortwave looks to stall over the northern Rocky Mountains and \npossibly form a closed low circulation. Temperatures warm back \nup above normal next week into the mid to upper 70s by Tuesday \ninto Wednesday. Models are indicating a shortwave disturbance \naround the Wednesday into Thursday time frame that would bring \nthe potential for breezy winds and also a threat for showers and\nthunderstorms particularly over Northeast Washington and into \nthe Idaho Panhandle. /SVH",
